  "statement": "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\sin^{2}x\\,dx$.",
  "solution": "Use $\\sin^{2}x=\\tfrac12(1-\\cos 2x)$. Then $\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\sin^{2}x\\,dx=\\tfrac12\\int_{0}^{\\pi}(1-\\cos 2x)\\,dx =\\tfrac12\\left[x-\\tfrac12\\sin 2x\\right]_{0}^{\\pi}=\\tfrac12(\\pi-0)=\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}$.",
  "hints": [
    "Replace $\\sin^{2}x$ using the double-angle formula.",
    "Integrate the constant and cosine terms."
  ],
